Sample the color-relief DEM with nearest filtering and decode it in highp.

The color-relief layer sampled the terrain-RGB texture with linear filtering.
Interpolating the packed red, green and blue bytes does not interpolate the
elevation, and on PowerVR GPUs (Pixel 10 and 11) the filtered texels decode to
values outside every stop, so the layer drew nothing. hillshade already samples
the same DEM with nearest filtering.

The fragment shader also inherited `precision mediump float` from the prelude.
The decode sums terms near 10000, where a 16-bit float steps by 8, so declare
highp as hillshade_prepare does.

diff --git a/include/mln/shaders/gl/color_relief.hpp b/include/mln/shaders/gl/color_relief.hpp
index 64a42b3..332919e 100644
--- a/include/mln/shaders/gl/color_relief.hpp
+++ b/include/mln/shaders/gl/color_relief.hpp
@@ -35,7 +35,11 @@ void main() {
     if (a_pos.y > 32766.5) v_pos.y = 1.0;
 }
 )";
-    static constexpr const char* fragment = R"(layout(std140) uniform ColorReliefDrawableUBO {
+    static constexpr const char* fragment = R"(#ifdef GL_ES
+precision highp float;
+#endif
+
+layout(std140) uniform ColorReliefDrawableUBO {
     highp mat4 u_matrix;
 };
 
